    id: 'uzu12', title: 'At home', si: 'Уйда', color: '#B9770E', scene: 'uzhovli',
    desc: 'Rooms, the дастурхон, and being a good guest',
    guide: [
      { h: 'Welcome: Келинг!', p: 'Hosts greet you with Келинг, келинг! (come in, come in) and марҳамат (please, go ahead). Answer with Ассалому алайкум and a hand on your heart.' },
      { h: 'Shoes off', p: 'Take your shoes off at the door; you may be offered slippers. If you are not sure, ask Оёқ кийимни ечайми?' },
      { h: 'The дастурхон', p: 'Guests sit at the тўр, the place of honour farthest from the door. The spread is ready before you arrive, and food keeps coming: say Тўйдим (I\'m full) or Бўлди (that\'s enough), and expect to be urged anyway.' },
      { h: 'Bread and tea', p: 'Bread is treated with respect: never put it upside down or on the floor, and break it by hand. Tea comes in a пиёла, poured a little at a time; a half-full bowl means "stay, I\'ll keep pouring".' },
      { h: 'Russian words at home', p: 'зал (Uzbek: меҳмонхона), свет (электр), стул (курси). Many people also say кухня for ошхона. The fridge is usually музхона (also холодильник; the dictionary word is музлатгич), and when the power is out, people say Свет йўқ.' },
    ],
